		<h1>Mox alternative.<br/><span>From minimal binary to multi-tenant platform.</span></h1>
		<p class="alt-lead">
			Mox is a genuinely lovely piece of engineering — one MIT-licensed Go binary, zero
			dependencies, best-in-class transport security (DANE, MTA-STS, DNSSEC), built-in
			webmail, and even a transactional HTTP API with webhooks. Vectis Mail overlaps that
			transactional surface, but plays a different position: a multi-tenant platform on the
			proven Postfix/Dovecot/Rspamd stack. Here's the honest comparison.
		</p>
		<div class="alt-meta">
			<span>Last updated <time datetime={dateModified}>14 June 2026</time></span>
			<span>·</span>
			<span>Written by Ian Holt</span>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 2rem;">
		<h2>At a glance</h2>
		<p class="subtitle">Side-by-side, current as of June 2026. Mox wins several rows — we call them out honestly.</p>
		<div class="compare-table-wrap">
			<table class="compare-table">
				<thead>
					<tr><th></th><th>Vectis Mail</th><th>Mox</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><th scope="row">Mail engine</th><td><strong>Postfix · Dovecot · Rspamd</strong> (decades-hardened)</td><td>From-scratch Go (single binary)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Footprint</th><td>Container stack (Postgres, Traefik, API)</td><td><strong>One binary, zero dependencies</strong></td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Protocols</th><td>SMTP · IMAP · POP3</td><td>SMTP · IMAP4 (+ webmail)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Transactional API</th><td>REST sending API — domain-scoped keys, batch, attachments</td><td>webapi (HTTP/JSON send + account ops)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Webhooks</th><td>Yes — HMAC-signed inbound + delivery events</td><td>Yes — incoming + outgoing delivery events</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Webmail</th><td><strong>Roundcube bundled</strong></td><td><strong>Built-in webmail</strong></td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Transport security</th><td>TLS 1.2+ · DKIM/SPF/DMARC</td><td><strong>+ DANE · MTA-STS · DNSSEC · TLSRPT · REQUIRETLS</strong></td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Spam filtering</th><td>Rspamd (mature, scriptable)</td><td>Built-in reputation + per-user Bayesian</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Antivirus</th><td>ClamAV (bundled, optional)</td><td>None bundled</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Multi-tenant / RBAC</th><td><strong>Yes</strong> — RBAC, domain-scoped keys, React admin</td><td>Multiple domains/accounts; single-operator focus</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Per-domain analytics + IP warmup</th><td><strong>Yes</strong> (Pro) — analytics, 30-day warmup ramp</td><td>Reputation tracking; no warmup scheduler</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Config model</th><td>Declarative <code>config.yaml</code> + <code>vectis apply</code></td><td>Quickstart-generated config file</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Update model</th><td><strong>Atomic 6-phase orchestrator</strong> with auto-rollback</td><td>Replace binary + restart</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">License</th><td>BSL 1.1 (auto-Apache 2.0 after 4y) + commercial Pro</td><td><strong>MIT</strong></td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Commercial support</th><td>Pro $39/tenant/mo · Enterprise from $499 + SLA</td><td>Community (single maintainer)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Maturity</th><td>v0.1.x — production since April 2026</td><td>Modern, well-tested, single-maintainer project</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>The verdict</h2>
		<div class="verdict-grid">
			<div class="verdict-card verdict-win">
				<h3>Choose Vectis Mail when…</h3>
				<ul>
					<li>You're running mail for many domains or customers and want multi-tenant RBAC and domain-scoped API keys.</li>
					<li>You want Rspamd + ClamAV-grade antispam and antivirus, not a lightweight built-in filter.</li>
					<li>You want per-domain analytics and a real IP-warmup ramp for a fresh sending IP.</li>
					<li>You want orchestrated atomic updates with automatic rollback, backup/restore, and observability.</li>
					<li>You want a vendor-backed Pro / Enterprise tier with a support SLA.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="verdict-card verdict-stay">
				<h3>Choose Mox when…</h3>
				<ul>
					<li>You want the simplest possible server: one MIT-licensed Go binary, zero dependencies.</li>
					<li>You want best-in-class modern transport security (DANE, MTA-STS, DNSSEC, TLSRPT, REQUIRETLS) out of the box.</li>
					<li>It's a personal or small-team server and you don't need multi-tenant admin or heavy AV.</li>
					<li>You value a permissive MIT licence over a source-available BSL.</li>
					<li>You like that upgrades are just "download the new binary and restart."</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>How they differ</h2>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Both have a transactional API — really</h3>
		<p>
			It's worth saying plainly, because it's easy to overclaim: Mox is <em>not</em> just an
			SMTP/IMAP server. It ships a <strong>webapi</strong> (a simple HTTP/JSON API to send
			messages without composing MIME or speaking SMTP) and both incoming and outgoing
			<strong>webhooks</strong> for delivery events. That's the same job Vectis Mail's
			transactional API does. So "self-hosted transactional email" is not a Vectis-only
			pitch against Mox — they genuinely overlap.
		</p>
		<p>
			The difference is the platform around the API. Vectis Mail adds domain-scoped API keys,
			per-domain rate limits and analytics, multi-tenant RBAC, and an admin UI for managing
			many domains and mailboxes. Mox keeps the surface small and operator-centric. If you
			want an API key per customer domain with usage analytics, that's Vectis; if you want one
			tidy server with an API for your own app, Mox does that beautifully.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Transport security: Mox is ahead</h3>
		<p>
			We won't pretend otherwise: Mox's modern transport-security story is excellent and
			currently ahead of Vectis. DANE, MTA-STS, DNSSEC, TLSRPT and REQUIRETLS are first-class,
			which means verified, downgrade-resistant TLS for both inbound and outbound mail. Vectis
			Mail enforces TLS 1.2+ and ships DKIM/SPF/DMARC, but does not yet ship DANE or MTA-STS.
			If those standards are a priority for you, that's a real point for Mox.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Antispam and antivirus</h3>
		<p>
			Mox uses a built-in reputation tracker and a per-user Bayesian junk filter that learns
			from your (Non-)Junk classification — light, effective, and zero-config. Vectis Mail runs
			full Rspamd (scriptable rules, RBLs, greylisting, milter integration) plus optional
			ClamAV antivirus. For a personal mailbox, Mox's filter is plenty; for higher-volume or
			multi-tenant sending where you want to tune policy and scan attachments, Rspamd + ClamAV
			is the heavier hammer.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Operations and scale</h3>
		<p>
			Mox is deliberately low-maintenance: one binary, config generated by a quickstart,
			upgrades by replacing the binary. Vectis Mail is a container stack with a single
			declarative <code>config.yaml</code>, a 6-phase update orchestrator that snapshots and
			<strong>automatically rolls back</strong> on failure, scheduled backups, and built-in
			metrics. That's more moving parts — the trade-off for multi-tenant management, analytics,
			IP-warmup tracking, and reversible updates. Neither is a clustered HA system.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Licensing</h3>
		<p>
			Mox is MIT-licensed — maximally permissive. Vectis Mail is source-available under the
			Business Source License 1.1, which converts to Apache 2.0 four years after each release
			and carries a commercial Pro license for the paid features. If an unrestricted permissive
			licence matters to you, Mox wins that line; if you want a vendor-backed product with a
			support SLA behind it, that's the Vectis side.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Migrating from Mox to Vectis Mail</h2>
		<p>Both expose IMAP, so mailbox data moves with standard tooling:</p>
		<ol class="alt-steps">
			<li><strong>Stand up Vectis Mail</strong> on a fresh VPS via the <a href="/getting-started/installation/">installation guide</a>, DNS still on Mox.</li>
			<li><strong>Sync mailboxes</strong> with <code>imapsync</code> from Mox's IMAP to Vectis Mail's Dovecot IMAP.</li>
			<li><strong>Generate new DKIM keys</strong> on Vectis Mail and publish alongside the existing ones; run both in parallel during cutover.</li>
			<li><strong>Re-point your app</strong> from Mox's webapi to the Vectis sending API (issue a domain-scoped API key, update the endpoint and payload).</li>
			<li><strong>Cut DNS over</strong>: switch MX to Vectis Mail. If you relied on Mox's DANE/MTA-STS records, review your TLSA/policy records before the switch.</li>
			<li><strong>Verify + decommission</strong> after a few clean days.</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			Honest caveat: if DANE/MTA-STS is part of why you ran Mox, factor that into the decision —
			Vectis doesn't replace those today.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
		<div class="alt-faq">
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>What's the difference between Mox and Vectis Mail?</h3>
				<p>
					Mox is a minimal, MIT-licensed single Go binary with zero dependencies, built for
					low-maintenance self-hosted email with best-in-class transport security (DANE,
					MTA-STS, DNSSEC, TLSRPT, REQUIRETLS), built-in webmail, and an HTTP webapi plus
					webhooks. Vectis Mail is a multi-tenant platform on the proven
					Postfix/Dovecot/Rspamd stack, with RBAC, domain-scoped API keys, per-domain
					analytics, IP-warmup tracking, Rspamd + ClamAV, orchestrated atomic updates with
					rollback, and a commercial Pro/SLA tier.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does Mox have a transactional email API?</h3>
				<p>
					Yes. Mox ships a webapi — a simple HTTP/JSON API for sending messages without
					composing MIME or speaking SMTP — plus incoming and outgoing webhooks for delivery
					events (delivered, delayed, failed, suppressed) and incoming mail. This overlaps
					directly with Vectis Mail's transactional API, so it isn't a Vectis-only
					capability. The difference is the surrounding platform: domain-scoped keys,
					per-domain rate limits and analytics, and multi-tenant RBAC.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Is Mox more secure than Vectis Mail?</h3>
				<p>
					On modern SMTP transport security, Mox is ahead today: DANE, MTA-STS, DNSSEC,
					TLSRPT and REQUIRETLS are first-class. Vectis Mail enforces TLS 1.2+ and ships
					DKIM/SPF/DMARC but does not currently ship DANE/MTA-STS. Vectis counters with
					Rspamd + ClamAV for heavier content and attachment filtering. Different strengths.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Is Mox a good fit for multi-tenant or SaaS use?</h3>
				<p>
					Mox supports multiple domains and accounts, but it's designed around a
					low-maintenance single-operator server rather than a multi-tenant platform. Vectis
					Mail is built for the multi-tenant case: RBAC, domain-scoped API keys, per-domain
					analytics and spam controls, IP-warmup tracking, and a React admin UI. If you run
					mail for many domains or customers, that's the Vectis argument.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Why would I choose Mox over Vectis Mail?</h3>
				<p>
					You want the simplest possible setup — one MIT-licensed Go binary with no
					dependencies, upgraded by replacing the binary — best-in-class transport security
					defaults, and built-in webmail, for a personal or small-team server. You don't need
					multi-tenant RBAC, heavy antispam/antivirus, IP-warmup tooling, or a commercial
					support SLA. Mox's MIT licence is also more permissive than Vectis's BSL 1.1.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

